  "query_preview": "..\n(This evening through next Monday)\n\nWEATHER PATTERN OVERVIEW: \n\nWeak instability of up to 250 J/kg of CAPE across eastern White\nPine County will continue isolated chances for showers. A\nlightning strike or two cannot be ruled out, though chances remain\nbelow 10%. Hi-res guidance seems to be too quick in exiting \nmoisture from the weak surface low that is moving into central \nUtah. Remnant moisture on the water vapor loop and developing \ncumulus on visible satellite suggest isolated showers will \ncontinue along and east of US 93 through this afternoon. Increased\nPoPs in these areas for mentionable showers. \n\nOtherwise, upper-level ridging builds into the Great Basin through\nthe rest of the workweek, leading to continued warmth with record\nto near record high temperatures across the climate sites through\nFriday. Ely sees the greatest likelihood for record highs today\nand Thursday at around a 10 percent chance with current forecast\nhighs within a couple degrees of the record. \n\nFriday into the weekend, another upper low will cutoff and dig\nsouthward off the California Coast, with a secondary and stronger\ntrough quickly moving into the Pacific Northwest on its heels.\nThis will lead to stronger southwesterly flow into Saturday with\nbreezy to locally windy conditions with max 24-hr gusts of 30 to \n40 mph across northern and central Nevada with a 35% chance for \nwind gusts over 40 mph in Elko and a 75% chance in Ely. \n\n\nFORECAST CONFIDENCE & DEVIATIONS:\n\nIncrease PoPs in eastern White Pine County to account for\nlocalized convective activity this afternoon. \n\nHigh confidence in well-above normal temperatures through Friday\nwith moderate confidence in a cooler, wetter pattern into the\nweekend and early next week. Long range ensemble clusters in\ndisagreement on how the two aforementioned troughs interact with\nthe wetter cluster scenarios that combine the troughs across the\nGreat Basin bringing greater chances for measurable precipitation \nby late Sunday into Monday. Low chances for mountain snowfall if \nthe deeper trough outcome pans out with snow levels dropping below\n10k ft with increased moisture.",
